bodyText:
Arkansas's landlord-tenant statutes forbid a tenant to:
- deliberately or negligently destroy, deface, damage, impair or remove any part of the premises, or knowingly let anyone on the premises with the tenant's permission do so;
- disturb other tenants' peaceful enjoyment of the premises (the tenant must also make guests behave);
- unreasonably withhold consent to your entry to inspect, repair, supply services, investigate possible rule, lease or criminal violations, or show the unit;
- change the locks without your permission;
- unless otherwise agreed, use the unit other than as a dwelling unit or conduct or permit illegal activities there.

Using the premises as a common nuisance or for illegal gambling, prostitution or illegal alcohol sales supports a nuisance eviction.

A tenant who refuses or fails to pay rent when due and then willfully refuses to vacate after 10 days' written notice commits a misdemeanor, fined $1 to $25 for each day; whether this old criminal statute is still enforced is a case-law question, so leave it to prosecutors and use a civil eviction. A lease cannot make a tenant give up the right to call the police or other emergency help. Your lease may add further prohibitions, and the tagged prohibited-acts clause restates the core list.
